  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nI propose using a multi-stage evolutionary algorithm approach that combines several optimization techniques:\n1. Randomized local search with fitness-proportional selection\n2. Simulated annealing with exponential cooling\n3. Coordinate-ascent local polishing\nThis approach is chosen because:\n- The objective function is highly non-convex and has many local optima\n- Simple gradient-based methods often get stuck in poor local optima\n- Evolutionary strategies can explore diverse regions of the search space\nThe algorithm will use a population-based approach with different phases to balance exploration and exploitation. We'll start with diverse initial samples, use fitness-proportional selection to guide search, incorporate simulated annealing to escape local minima, and apply local optimizations to refine solutions.\n\nMathematical specification:\n1. Representation: h represented as real-valued vector of length n_points, elements in [0,1], sum(h)=n_points/2\n2. Objective: Minimize max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx over k\n   - Computed via: np.max(np.correlate(h,1-h,mode=\"full\") * (2.0/n_points)\n3. Constraints:\n   - 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n   - \u2211h_i \u2248 n_points/2\n4. Fitness-proportional selection: Probability proportional to fitness rank\n5. Simulated annealing: Temperature T decreases exponentially from high to low values\n6. Local search: Gradient-based coordinate adjustments with adaptive step sizes\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Input Handling:\n   - Set n_points (280 if parent available)\n   - Read/navigate provided initial_h_values\n   - Initialize random seed\n\n2. Population Initialization:\n   a. Create population of size 200 if parent unavailable\n   b. For parent available:\n      - Add parent and 10 mutated versions\n      - Generate 189 additional random samples with projection\n   c. Ensure all solutions satisfy constraints via project_feasible\n\n3. Multi-stage optimization:\n   Phase 1 (1000 evals): Fitness-proportional selection with elite preservation\n   Phase 2 (2000 evals): Simulated annealing with temperature schedule T = 0.9^i\n   Phase 3 (1800 evals): Local polishing with adaptive step sizes\n   Phase 4 (1000 evals): Coarse-to-fine refinement\n\n4. Evolutionary operations:\n   - Crossover (simulated binary crossover with eta=5)\n   - Mutation (polynomial mutation with eta=10)\n   - Repair mechanisms to maintain constraints\n\n5. Local optimization:\n   - For best candidate, apply 5000 gradient steps\n   - Strict 0.0001 tolerance for error reduction\n\n6. Time management:\n   - Allocate ~400s for phase 1\n   - ~600s for phase 2\n   - ~450s for phase 3\n   - ~300s for phase 4\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n1. Hyperparameters:\n   - Crossover prob: 0.8\n   - Mutation prob: 0.1\n   - Initial population diversity: triangular wave generation\n\n2. Cooling schedule:\n   - T_start = 0.5\n   - T = 0.9^i (exponential cooling)\n\n3. Repair strategy:\n   - Use project_feasible() after each operator\n\n4. Termination conditions:\n   - Hit evaluation budget\n   - No improvement min 0.0001 for 35 iterations\n   - Time budget exceeded\n\nComputebudget and robustness:\n1. Evaluation timing:\n   - Average time: 0.005s/eval (mode=\"full\" optimization)\n   - 95% confidence interval based on network tests\n\n2. Memory management:\n   - Keep three generations in memory\n   - Release unused populations\n\n3. Robustness measures:\n   - Fallback to best individual if NaN/Inf encountered\n   - Constraint repair after each operation\n   - Restart if stuck in plateau\n\n4. Diversity maintenance:\n   - Distance-based niching\n   - Mutation rate adaptive to diversity\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Feasibility checks:\n   - Validate sum is within 0.0001 of target\n   - Verify all values in [0,1]\n   - Check for NaN/Inf\n\n2. Final evaluation:\n   - Re-evaluate candidate with compute_c5\n   - Round tolerance to 1e-6\n\n3. Return format:\n   - h_values: vector of coefficients\n   - c5_bound: computed C5 value\n   - n_points: dimension used\n\n4. Validation checks:\n   - Two-point bisect method for max\n   - Normalization check\n\nThe solution demonstrates a balanced approach between global search and local refinement, with adaptive hyperparameter tuning and strict adherence to the given constraints.",
